# Working with the acyclic tracker (acyclic [version])

This block matches the installed `acyclic`; run `acyclic prime` again after an upgrade rather than relying on an
older copy.

## The loop

Set `ACYCLIC_ACTOR` first (see "Who you are"), then:

```sh
acyclic ready
acyclic claim <thread>
# ...work its ready items in order, finishing each:
acyclic done <item> --repository <record> --commit <id>
acyclic done <item> --note "what was done"
# ...and when none of its items is left ready:
acyclic release <thread>
```

A thread is the unit of work: you claim a whole thread, and you and your subagents work every member without
claiming items. `ready` lists threads with ready work, best first, each with its ready `items` in order. Finish with
the `--repository` and `--commit` form, or with `--note` when the result isn't a commit.

## Setting up a clone

`acyclic init --replica <id> <path> --remote <url>` creates a tracker clone at `<path>` as a new replica, with the
shared repository as its `origin`. `acyclic sync` then pulls in what other machines recorded and pushes yours.

## Who you are

- Set `ACYCLIC_ACTOR` to a name unique to this session, such as `session/<machine>-<session id>`, or `run/<id>`
  for a run. Two sessions sharing a name share claims, and both would work the same thread. Without it you act as
  `agent/undeclared`.
- You are never the user. `--as-user` is for the user alone; don't pass it.

## Links

`acyclic link add plant waits-for beds` means plant can't start until beds is done. Links read left to right:

- `waits-for` holds work back.
- `relates-to` is context only.

Only `waits-for` orders work: ids, numbering and thread membership never do, so link every dependency you mean.

## Shaping the plan

`acyclic item add` and `acyclic item edit` take the same field flags:

- `--field <VALUE>` sets a scalar field, or adds to a set; repeat it for more.
- `--no-field` clears a scalar field and takes no value. `--no-field <VALUE>` removes from a set; repeat it for more.
- Naming `--field` and `--no-field` together is a usage error.

For example, `acyclic item edit beds --thread garden --no-notes --area soil --no-area clay` moves `beds` into the
`garden` thread, clears its notes, adds the area `soil` and removes the area `clay`. Thread membership is
`acyclic item edit --thread` (`--no-thread` takes the item out of its thread).

`acyclic item` also has `reopen`, `archive` and `delete`; `delete` refuses an item with children, links, evidence
or someone else's live claim on its thread, and in an approved thread only the user may delete. `acyclic link` has
`add` and `remove`. `acyclic import <file>` applies a whole plan document, and `--dry-run` reports what it would
change. `acyclic import --schema` prints the plan format.

Before adding work, read the plan with `acyclic list` and `acyclic thread list`, and give the item the parent, links
and thread it belongs with.

`acyclic graph --file <FILE>` writes the graph as DOT or Mermaid; on a large tracker, read the file rather than
standard output.

## Output

- `prime`, `completions`, `import --schema`, help and version text, and `graph` without `--file`, print documents
  or text. Every other command prints one JSON document when its output is piped, as a tool's is:
  `{"ok": true, "data": …}` or `{"ok": false, "error": {"code", "message", "details"}}`, errors included. Branch on
  `error.code`, not on the message. At a terminal it prints text for people instead; if your commands run in one,
  set `ACYCLIC_OUTPUT=json`.
- A bare group such as `acyclic item` prints its help.
- A command line that doesn't parse prints its error as text on standard error, with exit code 2; run
  `acyclic <command> --help` to see the command's arguments. `acyclic help <command>` shows them too.
- Records carry their own id as `id`. `list` and `show` return the same rows, and `show` adds details. Entries in
  `needs` embed the rows they are about.
- Times are RFC 3339 in UTC, such as `2026-10-15T09:30:00Z`.
- Exit codes: 0 done, 1 refused, 2 usage, 3 not found, 4 store (a lock, lost races or git; retry later),
  5 not set up, 6 unreadable state (run `acyclic doctor`).
- Writes report `changed`. A repeated command changes nothing of its own; it can still commit when a claim's lease
  is due for renewal.

## Finding and doing work

1. `acyclic ready` lists the threads you may work: yours first, then those nobody holds, approved first, then by
   title. Each row is the thread's summary with its ready `items`, each saying whether the user approved its work;
   `acyclic ready --approved` lists only approved work. An item in no thread is
   never ready, and `acyclic needs` lists it as `unthreaded`. `acyclic list` lists every item. `acyclic show
   <item>...` explains items, as an array in the order given: their readiness, children and their thread's claims.
   See "Details" for work that waits as `in-review` or `unverified`.
2. `acyclic claim <thread>` before starting. A claim from anything but a run holds a time lease (60 minutes by
   default, `--lease <minutes>` up to 480). Your claim lapses after its lease unless you keep writing to the tracker,
   which renews it at each write while you work. Don't use `--force` to take someone else's work. If `claim`, `ready`
   or `acyclic thread show <thread>` says another holder wins a contest for your thread, stop and run `acyclic release
   <thread>`: it drops only your claim. A claim refused with readiness `resource-busy` means another thread's claim
   holds a resource one of your thread's open items names: wait for that work, and don't force it. A lapsed lease
   frees your thread and its resources, so before work that needs a resource after a pause, check that `acyclic
   thread show <thread>` still names you as holder.
3. `acyclic done <item> --repository <record> --commit <id>` when an item is finished, or `--note` when the result
   isn't a commit. It finishes only what your thread holds, or what is ready, and a parent once its children are done;
   don't use `--force` to finish someone else's work. Finished items hold no resources. `acyclic release <thread>`
   once none of its items is left ready.
4. `acyclic thread` and `acyclic repo` keep threads and repository records; an item's thread and repositories are set
   with `acyclic item edit --thread` and `--repository`. `acyclic thread list` lists the threads with their counts, and
   `acyclic thread show <thread>` lists each member with its state and readiness. Both list a thread's `heads`: its
   open leaves that nothing in the thread orders. Two or more mean they may run in any order; when one needs another,
   link them.
5. Grouping work into threads is yours: create threads with `acyclic thread add`, fill them with `acyclic item edit
   --thread`, give each a title and brief, and leave approving them to the user. New or moved work joins the thread
   its parent passes down, or else the one its siblings share; an add, a move under another parent or an import
   reports it as `placed`. Once the user approves a thread, your change to its members or repositories doesn't
   apply unless you are a run on a project-management thread: it waits in `acyclic needs` as a proposal for the user.
   An item or thread edit, and an import, list their proposals in `proposals`, each with the `field` it proposed.
   Carry on with other work.
6. `acyclic sync` exchanges work with the tracker's shared repository (`origin`): it fetches every replica's state,
   joins it, and pushes yours; `changed` says whether joining changed this clone and `pushed` counts the refs sent. Run
   it before reading what another machine wrote and after writing what one should see. With
   `git config acyclic.pushAfterWrite true` in the tracker clone, every write pushes too and reports `push`; a push that
   fails leaves the write in place and shows in `acyclic needs` until a sync. In a run's clone, where
   `acyclic.operations` names the run, writes queue as operations instead: `acyclic ops push` sends them, and `sync`
   pushes nothing.

## Details

A done item counts for what waits on it from another thread, or outside any thread, only once each commit in its
evidence is on that repository's default branch, as last fetched in your clone (named in
`~/.config/acyclic/clones.toml`). Until then they wait on it as `in-review`; run `git fetch` there before
`acyclic ready`. They wait as `unverified`, with a reason, when the commit can't be checked, such as with no clone
mapped: fix the mapping, since fetching won't help. Work finished without a commit counts at once.
